n"} {"question" "q-338142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-338142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/state.json" "file_sha256" "01c01a26e01298a5b694130ac4e21b96a9528a36eca715d82138edd53e7bf7d5" "basis" 2523 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-337547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+4y=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(x{e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{2x})^{′}(x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x})-(2{e}^{2x})(x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}+2x{e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot x{e}^{2x}}{{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}}{{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|)({e}^{2x})+(-\\frac{1}{x})(x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+1) \\\\  & =(A-1){e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-1)\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}{{\\,-\\,{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f01b5b53dc391bfb150476d8d9ef0bc214ec536c5cc085a646b8ee35b35459f"} {"question" "q-337547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2x{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|"] ["math" "-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|"] ["math" "-4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|"] ["math" "-x{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|"] ["math" "-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-12y=14x{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{-7x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=14x{e}^{-4x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{-4x})^{′}({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(3{e}^{3x})-(-4{e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-x}+4{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =7{e}^{-x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{-4x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x{e}^{-7x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2})({e}^{-4x})+[-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}]({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(A-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-\\frac{2}{49})\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}{{-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f01b5b53dc391bfb150476d8d9ef0bc214ec536c5cc085a646b8ee35b35459f"} {"question" "q-337541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{5}){e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{3x}{7}){e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{3}){e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{7}){e}^{-4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(x{e}^{-x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}(x{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})-(-{e}^{-x})(x{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}-x{e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{-2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\cdot x{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{3}{e}^{-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{-x}}{{x}^{3}{e}^{-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}){e}^{-x}+(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})x{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{x}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+{{\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f01b5b53dc391bfb150476d8d9ef0bc214ec536c5cc085a646b8ee35b35459f"} {"question" "q-337548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{4x}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{4{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337548" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y=2x{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{-x}dx=-(x+1){e}^{-x}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x{e}^{2x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})({e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{2x})^{′}({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(3{e}^{3x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{5x}-2{e}^{5x} \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{2x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{{e}^{5x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{2x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}}{{e}^{5x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x{e}^{-x}\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2})({e}^{2x})+[-2(x+1){e}^{-x}({e}^{3x})] \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}-2(x+1){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+2){e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+2x+2){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =(A-2){e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-2)\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}\\,{{-\\,({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337542" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f01b5b53dc391bfb150476d8d9ef0bc214ec536c5cc085a646b8ee35b35459f"} {"question" "q-337542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-(2{x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}"] ["math" "-(2{x}^{2}+4x){e}^{2x}"] ["math" "-({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}"] ["math" "-({x}^{2}+4x){e}^{2x}"] ["math" "-({x}^{3}+2x){e}^{2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337542" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y=\\frac{2{e}^{3x}}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(x{e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}(x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x})-(3{e}^{3x})(x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}+3x{e}^{6x}-3x{e}^{6x} \\\\  & ={e}^{6x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{2{e}^{3x}\\cdot x{e}^{3x}}{(1+{x}^{2}){e}^{6x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}) \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{2{e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{(1+{x}^{2}){e}^{6x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we needed to use substitution $t=1+{x}^{2}$ to find ${u}_{1}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))+(x{e}^{3x})(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{3x}+Bx{e}^{3x}+{{{e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)={e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337391" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f01b5b53dc391bfb150476d8d9ef0bc214ec536c5cc085a646b8ee35b35459f"} {"question" "q-337391" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337391" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))"] ["math" "{e}^{3x}(x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))"] ["math" "{e}^{3x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))"] ["math" "{e}^{3x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))"] ["math" "{e}^{3x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337391" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{3x}(2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}))"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y={csc}^{3}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x=2{cos}^{2}⁡2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={csc}^{3}⁡2x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡2x+2{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {csc}^{2}⁡2x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{{sin}^{3}⁡2x}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then $du=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,dx,$ so $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\,du,$ and the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{{sin}^{3}⁡2x}\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,du) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{-3}\\,du \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}{u}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8{sin}^{2}⁡2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(-\\frac{1}{8{sin}^{2}⁡2x})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2x}{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}-\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}(2{cos}^{2}⁡2x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,+\\,{{\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f01b5b53dc391bfb150476d8d9ef0bc214ec536c5cc085a646b8ee35b35459f"} {"question" "q-338528" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338528" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338528" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following results (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-337950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡2x+2{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(B-\\frac{1}{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $B$ as\n\n$$\nB:=(B-\\frac{1}{2})\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,{{-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/1