l "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+5y=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following results (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=x{e}^{2x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{e}^{4x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{e}^{4x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,(x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =x{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{{x{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f01b5b53dc391bfb150476d8d9ef0bc214ec536c5cc085a646b8ee35b35459f"} {"question" "q-338767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "4x{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "x{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x{e}^{2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+5y=4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{cos}^{2}⁡2x-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}({cos}^{2}⁡2x+{sin}^{2}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2\\int dx \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338142" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f01b5b53dc391bfb150476d8d9ef0bc214ec536c5cc085a646b8ee35b35459f"} {"question" "q-338142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}"] ["math" "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}"] ["math" "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}"] ["math" "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}"] ["math" "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338142" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{e}^{-2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{e}^{-2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int dx \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|)({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(x{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5f01b5b53dc391bfb150476d8d9ef0bc214ec536c5cc085a646b8ee35b35459f"} {"question" "q-338774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] ["math" "x{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)"] ["math" "{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] ["math" "{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] ["math" "{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "q-338774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/state.json" "file_sha256" "35b93120982cd70273c914d2533a9443e3b31ec8494d9f364450f0c4e450a779"} {"question" "e-6618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny'' - 2y' + y = 18x e^{x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x} + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/example-6618.json" "file_sha256" "c450c788d39a28f31e416f3267b689af7371860ae8d81c84dec126c1ba81d3a1"} {"question" "e-6618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ may be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x) = -\\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x) = \\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}+x{e}^{2x}-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nNow compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-18\\int {x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =18\\int x\\,dx \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-6{x}^{3}){e}^{x}+(9{x}^{2})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3}{e}^{x}+9{x}^{3}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+{{3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 3x^3 e^{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/example-6618.json" "file_sha256" "c450c788d39a28f31e416f3267b689af7371860ae8d81c84dec126c1ba81d3a1"} {"question" "e-6617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+y=\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan x \\sin x\\,\\textrm{d}x\n= -\\sin x + \\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/example-6617.json" "file_sha256" "e7a0ca8b7db79f4e620f70f159515a01c6adafb418df687a4635deea44589579"} {"question" "e-6617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=\\tan x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\cos x,\n\\qquad\ny_2(x)=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|)+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\,{{-\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071118/example-6617.json" "file_sha256" "e7a0ca8b7db79f4e620f70f159515a01c6adafb418df687a4635deea44589579"}] "usage" {"q-338142" [] "q-337391" [] "q-337950" [] "q-338767" [] "e-6617" [] "e-6618" []} "basis" 2559 "retractions" [[17592186229661 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n$y^{″} - 6y^{′} + 9y = \\frac{2e^{3x}}{1 + x^{2}}$.\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x) = Ae^{3x} + Bxe^{3x} + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method.*"] [17592186229665 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n$y^{″} + 4y = 2\\tan x$.\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x) = A\\cos 2x + B\\sin 2x + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following results (integration constants omitted):*\n$∫\\sin 2x\\tan xdx|= x - \\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2x; ∫\\cos 2x\\tan xdx|= \\ln \\mid \\cos x \\mid - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos 2x - \\frac{1}{2}$"] [17592186228903 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n$y'' - 2y' + y = 18x e^{x}$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x} + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)$?"] [17592186228903 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ may be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}(x)y_{1}(x) + u_{2}(x)y_{2}(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{u_{1}^{′}y_{1}^{′} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2}^{′} = f(x); u_{1}^{′}y_{1} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x) = -\\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx, \\qquad u_2(x) = \\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = x e^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= y_{1}y_{2}^{′} - y_{1}^{′}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (e^{x})(xe^{x})^{′} - (e^{x})^{′}(xe^{x}) \\\\\n&= (e^{x})(e^{x} + xe^{x}) - (e^{x})(xe^{x}) \\\\\n&= e^{2x} + xe^{2x} - xe^{2x} \\\\\n&= e^{2x} \\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nNow compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1}(x) &= - ∫\\frac{18xe^{x} \\cdot xe^{x}}{e^{2x}}dx \\\\\n&=-18∫x^{2}dx \\\\\n&=-6x^{3} \\\\\nu_{2}(x) &= ∫\\frac{18xe^{x} \\cdot e^{x}}{e^{2x}}dx \\\\\n&= 18∫xdx \\\\\n&= 9x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}y_{1} + u_{2}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (-6x^{3})e^{x} + (9x^{2})(xe^{x}) \\\\\n&=-6x^{3}e^{x} + 9x^{3}e^{x} \\\\\n&= 3x^{3}e^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y_{c}(x) + y_{p}(x) \\\\\n&= Ae^{x} + Bxe^{x} + 3x^{3}e^{x}_(⏟)_(g(x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 3x^3 e^{x}\n$$"] [17592186228902 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n$y''+y=\\tan{x}$.\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n$y(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x)$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)$?\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n$\\int \\tan x \\sin x\\,\\textrm{d}x = -\\sin x + \\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|$"] [17592186228902 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}(x)y_{1}(x) + u_{2}(x)y_{2}(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{u_{1}^{′}y_{1}^{′} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2}^{′} = f(x); u_{1}^{′}y_{1} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x, \\qquad u_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\tan x\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\cos x, \\qquad y_2(x)=\\sin x\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= y_{1}(x)y_{2}^{′}(x) - y_{1}^{′}(x)y_{2}(x) \\\\\n&= (\\cos x)(\\sin x)^{′} - (\\cos x)^{′}(\\sin x) \\\\\n&= (\\cos x)(\\cos x) - (-\\sin x)(\\sin x) \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} x + \\sin^{2} x \\\\\n&= 1 \\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1}(x) &= - ∫(f(x)y_{2}(x))/(W(y_{1}, y_{2}))dx \\\\\n&= - ∫\\tan x\\sin xdx \\\\\n&= \\sin x - \\ln \\mid \\tan x + \\sec x \\mid \\\\\nu_{2}(x) &= ∫(f(x)y_{1}(x))/(W(y_{1}, y_{2}))dx \\\\\n&= ∫\\tan x\\cos xdx \\\\\n&= ∫\\sin xdx \\\\\n&=-\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(x) &= u_{1}y_{1} + u_{2}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (\\cos x)(\\sin x - \\ln\\begin{vmatrix}\\tan x + \\sec x &) + (-\\cos x)(\\sin x) \\\\ =-\\cos x\\ln & \\tan x + \\sec x\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y_{c}(x) + y_{p}(x) \\\\\n&= A\\cos x + B\\sin x- \\cos x\\ln \\mid \\tan x + \\sec x \\mid _(⏟)_(g(x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186228387 17592186228388 17592186228389 17592186228390 17592186228391 17592186228409 17592186228410 17592186228411 17592186228412 17592186228413 17592186369114 17592186369115 17592186369116 17592186369117 17592186369118 17592186369121 17592186369122 17592186369123 17592186369124 17592186369125] "immutable_fields" [[17592186228386 :answer-field/correct] [17592186228386 :answer-field/id] [17592186228386 :answer-field/key] [17592186228386 :answer-field/type] [17592186228408 :answer-field/correct] [17592186228408 :answer-field/id] [17592186228408 :answer-field/key] [17592186228408 :answer-field/type] [17592186369113 :answer-field/correct] [17592186369113 :answer-field/id] [17592186369113 :answer-field/key] [17592186369113 :answer-field/type] [17592186369120 :answer-field/correct] [17592186369120 :answer-field/id] [17592186369120 :answer-field/key] [17592186369120 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
